<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Ecommerce</span>
      <h1>Shopify vs BigCommerce: Best Ecommerce Platform 2026</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Shopify and BigCommerce are the two leading fully hosted ecommerce platforms that merchants shortlist in 2026, and choosing between them is rarely obvious. Shopify wins on ease of use, app ecosystem size, and a conversion-optimized checkout. BigCommerce wins on built-in features, no transaction fees, and stronger out-of-the-box SEO. This comparison breaks down hosting, pricing, themes, apps, SEO, payments, and scalability so you can pick the right hosted platform for your store.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.shopify.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Shopify Free</a>
      <a href="https://www.bigcommerce.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try BigCommerce Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Shopify</th><th>BigCommerce</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Beginners, fast launch, large app ecosystem</td><td>Feature-rich stores, B2B, no transaction fees</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Starting Price</td><td>$29/mo (Basic)</td><td>$29.95/mo (Standard)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hosting</td><td>Included, fully managed</td><td>Included, fully managed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SSL Certificate</td><td>Included, auto-renewed</td><td>Included, auto-renewed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Themes</td><td>12 free, 100+ paid</td><td>12 free (Stencil), 100+ paid</td></tr>
        <tr><td>App Store</td><td>8,000+ apps</td><td>1,300+ apps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Payment Gateways</td><td>Shopify Payments + 100+</td><td>65+ gateways, no fees</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transaction Fees</td><td>0% on Shopify Payments, up to 2% otherwise</td><td>0% on every gateway</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SEO Control</td><td>Solid, limited URL structure</td><td>Strong, custom URLs, auto redirects</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scalability</td><td>Automatic, no sales caps</td><td>Automatic, annual sales thresholds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blog Engine</td><td>Basic built-in blog</td><td>Built-in WordPress-style blog</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile App</td><td>iOS, Android for store management</td><td>iOS, Android for store management</td></tr>
        <tr><td>B2B Features</td><td>Limited, requires apps</td><td>Built-in B2B edition</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Multi-Channel</td><td>Shop, Amazon, eBay, social</td><td>Amazon, eBay, Google, social</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Open Source</td><td>No, proprietary SaaS</td><td>No, proprietary SaaS</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Shopify?</h2>
    <p>Shopify is a fully hosted ecommerce platform launched in 2006 by Tobias Lutke, Daniel Weinand, and Scott Lake. Originally built to power an online snowboard store, Shopify has grown into the most popular hosted ecommerce solution in the world, powering over 4 million stores across 175 countries. The core pitch is simple: you sign up, pick a theme, add products, and your store is live. No servers to manage, no software to install, no SSL certificates to configure. The platform is optimized for speed to launch and ease of use, which is why it dominates the small to mid-market segment.</p>
    <p>The current pricing starts at $29 per month for the Basic plan, which includes hosting, SSL, unlimited products, and access to the Shopify App Store with over 8,000 apps. Higher tiers add advanced reporting, lower transaction fees, and staff accounts. Shopify also offers Shopify Plus starting at $2,300 per month for enterprise merchants doing high volume. The defining technical feature is that Shopify handles the entire infrastructure stack, so you never touch a server. The tradeoff is that you are locked into their ecosystem and have less control over deep customization, plus you face transaction fees if you do not use Shopify Payments.</p>

    <h2>What Is BigCommerce?</h2>
    <p>BigCommerce is a fully hosted ecommerce platform founded in 2009 by Mitchell Harper and Eddie Machalaani and headquartered in Austin, Texas. Unlike Shopify, which targets ease of use above all, BigCommerce positions itself as the feature-rich hosted platform for merchants who want more functionality out of the box without relying on third-party apps. BigCommerce powers tens of thousands of stores in over 120 countries and is particularly popular with B2B merchants, multi-brand retailers, and stores doing mid-six to eight figures in annual revenue.</p>
    <p>The current pricing starts at $29.95 per month for the Standard plan, which includes hosting, SSL, unlimited products, professional reports, and no transaction fees on any payment gateway. Higher tiers add customer groups, stored cards, Google customer reviews, and custom SSL. BigCommerce also offers BigCommerce Enterprise for high-volume merchants with custom pricing. The defining technical feature is that BigCommerce bundles advanced functionality such as faceted search, product variants, and real-time carrier shipping into every plan, reducing the need for paid apps. The tradeoff is that BigCommerce enforces annual sales thresholds on each plan, which means a successful year may push you into a higher pricing tier automatically.</p>

    <h2>Hosting and Performance</h2>
    <p>Hosting is one area where Shopify and BigCommerce are essentially tied, because both platforms are fully hosted and include enterprise-grade infrastructure on every plan. Neither platform requires you to manage servers, configure SSL, or worry about bandwidth limits. The differences are subtle and live in performance details and how each platform scales during traffic spikes.</p>

    <h3>Shopify Managed Infrastructure</h3>
    <p>Shopify includes hosting on every plan, starting at $29 per month. The infrastructure is built on Google Cloud and handles billions of dollars in transactions each year. Shopify offers automatic scaling during traffic spikes, a global CDN, and a stated 99.99% uptime. You never see a server, never configure a firewall, and never worry about a traffic surge crashing your store on Black Friday. Shopify's checkout is hosted on its own optimized infrastructure, which is one reason it converts so well. Page load speeds are consistently fast across all themes, and the platform handles image resizing and lazy loading automatically.</p>
    <p>The tradeoff is that you cannot choose your hosting location beyond a general region, and you cannot install custom server software. If you need a specific PHP extension, a custom caching layer, or a particular database configuration, Shopify does not allow it. For 95% of merchants, this is never a problem, but it can matter for highly specialized stores with custom data needs.</p>

    <h3>BigCommerce Managed Infrastructure</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce also includes hosting on every plan, starting at $29.95 per month. The infrastructure runs on Google Cloud and offers automatic scaling, a global CDN, and 99.99% uptime. BigCommerce has invested heavily in headless commerce support, which means you can use BigCommerce as the backend and connect a custom frontend built in Next.js, React, or any other framework. This is attractive for brands that want a unique storefront experience without sacrificing the hosted backend.</p>
    <p>BigCommerce page speeds are competitive with Shopify, and the Stencil theme framework is optimized for performance. The tradeoff is similar: you cannot choose your server location precisely or install custom server software. The bigger difference is that BigCommerce enforces annual sales thresholds on each plan, so a traffic surge that doubles your revenue may also bump you into a higher pricing tier, which is not a concern on Shopify.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for battle-tested infrastructure at extreme scale goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>. Best for headless commerce support and built-in performance features goes to <span class="winner-tag">BigCommerce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing and Transaction Fees</h2>
    <p>Pricing is where Shopify and BigCommerce diverge most sharply, even though their entry-level monthly fees are within a dollar of each other. The difference is in transaction fees and what features are included in each tier. Shopify charges transaction fees if you do not use Shopify Payments, while BigCommerce charges zero transaction fees on any gateway. However, BigCommerce enforces annual sales thresholds, while Shopify does not.</p>

    <h3>Shopify Pricing</h3>
    <p>Shopify Basic costs $29 per month and includes everything needed to run a store: hosting, SSL, unlimited products, two staff accounts, and Shopify Payments with 0% transaction fees. If you use a third-party gateway like PayPal or Stripe, Shopify charges an additional 2% transaction fee on top of the gateway's own fees. The Shopify plan at $79 per month adds gift cards, professional reports, and lower credit card rates. Advanced Shopify at $299 per month adds custom reports, the lowest credit card rates, and real-time carrier shipping.</p>
    <p>For most small to mid-size stores, the Basic plan at $29 is sufficient to start. The hidden cost is premium apps. A typical Shopify store spends $50 to $200 per month on apps for email marketing, reviews, SEO, and upsells, because many features that BigCommerce includes by default require a paid app on Shopify. There are no annual sales thresholds on any Shopify plan, which means your pricing stays predictable even as you scale.</p>

    <h3>BigCommerce Pricing</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce Standard costs $29.95 per month and includes hosting, SSL, unlimited products, professional reports, real-time carrier shipping, gift cards, and no transaction fees on any gateway. The Plus plan at $79.95 per month adds customer groups, stored cards, and Google customer reviews. The Pro plan at $299.95 per month adds custom SSL, product filtering, and abandoned cart saver. BigCommerce Enterprise offers custom pricing for high-volume merchants.</p>
    <p>The defining difference is that BigCommerce charges zero transaction fees on every gateway, which saves significant money for stores processing high volume on PayPal or Stripe. The tradeoff is the annual sales threshold: the Standard plan caps at $50,000 in annual sales, Plus at $180,000, and Pro at $400,000. If your store exceeds the threshold, you are automatically moved to the next tier. For fast-growing stores, this can mean unexpected price jumps. For stores with predictable revenue, the no-transaction-fee model is usually cheaper overall.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for predictable pricing and no sales caps goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>. Best for zero transaction fees and more built-in features per tier goes to <span class="winner-tag">BigCommerce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Themes and Design</h2>
    <p>Both platforms offer a similar selection of themes, but the design workflow and customization experience differ. Shopify themes are polished and easy to customize through a visual drag-and-drop editor. BigCommerce themes use the Stencil framework, which is also modern and mobile-responsive but has a slightly steeper learning curve for deep customization.</p>

    <h3>Shopify Theme Store</h3>
    <p>Shopify offers 12 free themes and over 100 paid themes ranging from $150 to $350 one-time. The free Dawn theme is well-designed and sufficient for most small stores. All Shopify themes are mobile-responsive, support drag-and-drop section editing, and are reviewed by Shopify before publication, which means they are less likely to break. The visual theme editor lets you customize colors, fonts, and layouts without touching code. Shopify themes are generally considered the most polished out-of-the-box among hosted platforms.</p>

    <h3>BigCommerce Stencil Themes</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce offers 12 free Stencil themes and over 100 paid themes from the BigCommerce Theme Marketplace, with prices ranging from $145 to $300 one-time. The free Cornerstone theme is a solid starting point. Stencil themes support drag-and-drop page building and are mobile-responsive. BigCommerce gives developers more access to theme code through the Stencil CLI, which allows local development and version control. The tradeoff is that the visual editor is slightly less intuitive than Shopify's, and the theme marketplace is smaller, which means fewer options for niche industries.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for polished out-of-the-box design and ease of customization goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>. Best for developer access and theme code control goes to <span class="winner-tag">BigCommerce</span>.</p>

    <h2>App and Integration Ecosystem</h2>
    <p>The app ecosystem is where Shopify has a decisive advantage. Shopify has over 8,000 apps in its App Store, while BigCommerce has around 1,300 apps. However, BigCommerce compensates by including many features as built-in functionality that would require paid apps on Shopify.</p>

    <h3>Shopify App Store</h3>
    <p>Shopify apps are installed with one click and are reviewed for quality and security. Popular categories include email marketing (Klaviyo), reviews (Judge.me), SEO (Booster SEO), upsells (ReConvert), and subscriptions (Recharge). Most apps charge a monthly fee ranging from $5 to $50. The advantage is that the ecosystem is deep, with multiple options for every niche. The disadvantage is that monthly app fees can add up to more than your Shopify subscription, and many features that BigCommerce includes by default require a paid app on Shopify.</p>

    <h3>BigCommerce App Marketplace</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce has a smaller but curated App Marketplace with around 1,300 apps. The advantage is that BigCommerce includes many features as built-in functionality, such as faceted search, product filtering, gift cards, real-time carrier shipping, and professional reports on every plan. This reduces the need for paid apps and lowers total monthly cost. The disadvantage is that for niche features like advanced subscriptions, complex bundles, or specialized shipping rules, the app ecosystem is thinner, and you may need to commission custom development.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for app variety and ecosystem depth goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>. Best for built-in features and lower app spend goes to <span class="winner-tag">BigCommerce</span>.</p>

    <h2>SEO and Content Marketing</h2>
    <p>Both platforms are SEO-capable, but BigCommerce has a slight edge because it offers more control over URL structure, automatic redirects, and structured data. Shopify SEO is solid but more constrained, with fixed URL structures that you cannot remove.</p>

    <h3>Shopify SEO</h3>
    <p>Shopify handles the technical SEO basics well: fast page loads, automatic SSL, clean URLs, and built-in schema markup for products. However, Shopify forces certain URL structures, such as appending /collections/ to category pages and /products/ to product pages. You cannot remove these, which some SEOs find frustrating. The built-in blog is functional but basic compared to WordPress or BigCommerce, with limited formatting options and no advanced taxonomy. Shopify also generates duplicate URLs for product variants, which can create crawl issues if not managed with canonical tags.</p>

    <h3>BigCommerce SEO Advantage</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce gives you more control over URL structure, including custom URLs for products and categories, and automatically generates 301 redirects when you change a URL. Built-in features include microdata, schema markup, AMP support, and a robust blog engine that is closer to WordPress in functionality. BigCommerce also handles product variants on a single URL, which avoids the duplicate content issues that Shopify can create. For stores that rely on organic search as a primary growth channel, BigCommerce offers more granular control without requiring third-party SEO apps.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for out-of-the-box technical SEO and URL control goes to <span class="winner-tag">BigCommerce</span>. Best for ease of use and basic SEO needs goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>.</p>

    <h2>Payments and Checkout</h2>
    <p>The checkout experience directly affects conversion rates, and this is where Shopify has a clear advantage. Shopify's checkout is one of the best-converting in the industry, while BigCommerce offers more gateway flexibility and zero transaction fees but a slightly less optimized checkout flow.</p>

    <h3>Shopify Checkout</h3>
    <p>Shopify's checkout is one of its biggest selling points. It is optimized for conversion, supports Shop Pay for one-click checkout, and includes accelerated checkouts like Apple Pay and Google Pay. Shopify Payments is the default gateway and charges 0% transaction fees on top of standard credit card rates. The checkout is hosted on Shopify's infrastructure, which means it loads fast and is always secure. Shopify has invested heavily in checkout optimization, including features like Shop Pay installments, dynamic checkout buttons, and AI-powered fraud analysis. The tradeoff is that Shopify charges up to 2% transaction fees if you use a third-party gateway instead of Shopify Payments.</p>

    <h3>BigCommerce Payment Flexibility</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce supports over 65 payment gateways, including Stripe, PayPal, Square, Authorize.net, and Adyen, with zero transaction fees on any gateway. The built-in checkout is functional and supports accelerated checkouts like Apple Pay and Google Pay. BigCommerce also offers a headless checkout option for enterprise merchants who want to build a custom checkout flow. The tradeoff is that the default BigCommerce checkout is slightly less conversion-optimized than Shopify's out of the box, and features like one-click checkout require a supported wallet or third-party integration. For merchants who need a specific gateway that Shopify does not support, or who want to avoid Shopify's transaction fees, BigCommerce is the better choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for conversion-optimized checkout goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>. Best for gateway flexibility and zero transaction fees goes to <span class="winner-tag">BigCommerce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Shopify Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Fastest time to launch for new stores</li>
        <li>Conversion-optimized checkout with Shop Pay</li>
        <li>8,000+ apps in the App Store</li>
        <li>Predictable pricing with no annual sales caps</li>
        <li>Polished visual theme editor for non-developers</li>
        <li>24/7 customer support on all plans</li>
        <li>Handles Black Friday traffic spikes without configuration</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Shopify Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Transaction fees up to 2% if not using Shopify Payments</li>
        <li>Monthly app fees can add up quickly</li>
        <li>Limited control over URL structure and deep customization</li>
        <li>Basic blog engine compared to BigCommerce or WordPress</li>
        <li>Many features require paid apps that BigCommerce includes</li>
        <li>Higher tiers get expensive for scaling businesses</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>BigCommerce Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Zero transaction fees on every payment gateway</li>
        <li>More built-in features per plan than Shopify</li>
        <li>Stronger out-of-the-box SEO with custom URLs and auto redirects</li>
        <li>Robust blog engine closer to WordPress</li>
        <li>Native B2B features and customer groups</li>
        <li>Headless commerce support on all plans</li>
        <li>Real-time carrier shipping and gift cards on Standard plan</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>BigCommerce Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Annual sales thresholds can force unexpected plan upgrades</li>
        <li>Smaller app ecosystem with around 1,300 apps</li>
        <li>Steeper learning curve for beginners</li>
        <li>Theme marketplace is smaller than Shopify's</li>
        <li>Default checkout is less optimized than Shopify's</li>
        <li>Higher-tier plans get expensive for scaling businesses</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>First-Time Store Owners</h3>
      <p>If you are launching your first online store and have no technical skills, Shopify is the better choice. You can go from sign-up to live store in a weekend. The managed hosting, curated themes, and one-click apps mean you spend time selling, not configuring settings. The $29 monthly fee is a reasonable price for the time you save, and the intuitive dashboard reduces the learning curve significantly.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>B2B and Wholesale Merchants</h3>
      <p>If you sell to other businesses, offer wholesale pricing, or need customer groups and stored payment cards, BigCommerce is the stronger choice. Built-in B2B features, customer groups, and quote management come native on the Plus and Pro plans. Shopify can handle B2B through apps or Shopify Plus, but the experience is less seamless and costs significantly more at the enterprise tier.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Multi-Channel Retailers</h3>
      <p>If you sell across Amazon, eBay, Google Shopping, and social platforms alongside your own store, both platforms handle multi-channel well. BigCommerce has slightly deeper native integrations with Amazon, eBay, and Google, while Shopify offers the Shop app and stronger social commerce on TikTok and Instagram. Choose based on which channels you prioritize.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Stores Processing High Volume on Third-Party Gateways</h3>
      <p>If you process significant volume on PayPal, Stripe, or another third-party gateway and want to avoid Shopify's transaction fees, BigCommerce is the better choice. A store doing $100,000 per month on PayPal saves $2,000 per month in Shopify transaction fees by using BigCommerce. The tradeoff is the annual sales threshold, which may push you into the Plus or Pro plan.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Brands That Want a Unique Storefront</h3>
      <p>If you want a custom storefront built in Next.js, React, or another modern framework, BigCommerce offers better headless commerce support on every plan. Shopify supports headless through Shopify Hydrogen and Storefront API, but the developer experience is more complex and the cost is higher. For brands that prioritize a unique frontend experience, BigCommerce is the more flexible choice.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Launching a 50-product fashion store in a weekend</h3>
      <p>A founder wants to launch a fashion store with 50 products before a trade show on Monday. Shopify lets them sign up on Friday, pick the Dawn theme, upload products via CSV, connect Shopify Payments, and go live by Sunday night. BigCommerce would also work, but the dashboard is slightly more complex and the theme customization takes longer for a first-timer. For speed to launch, Shopify wins.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Scaling a B2B parts distributor to $2M per month</h3>
      <p>A B2B parts distributor needs customer groups, custom pricing tiers, stored payment cards, and quote management. BigCommerce Plus handles all of this natively at $79.95 per month, while Shopify would require Shopify Plus at $2,300 per month plus paid B2B apps. The B2B merchant saves over $25,000 per year on platform fees with BigCommerce and gets a purpose-built B2B experience.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Running a Black Friday flash sale with 10,000 concurrent visitors</h3>
      <p>A store expects 10,000 concurrent visitors during a Black Friday flash sale. On Shopify, the platform scales automatically and the checkout stays fast with no configuration. On BigCommerce, the platform also scales automatically, but a successful sale may push the store past its annual sales threshold, triggering an automatic plan upgrade. For pure traffic spike handling with no surprises, Shopify is the safer choice.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Building a headless storefront for a heritage brand</h3>
      <p>A heritage brand wants a unique storefront built in Next.js with a custom design system, while keeping the backend on a hosted platform. BigCommerce offers headless commerce support on every plan with a robust Storefront API. Shopify supports headless through Hydrogen and the Storefront API, but the developer experience is more complex and enterprise-grade headless often requires Shopify Plus. For a brand that prioritizes a custom frontend, BigCommerce is the more cost-effective choice.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best for most merchants and beginners: Shopify.</strong> Shopify's polished checkout, intuitive dashboard, and 8,000+ app ecosystem make it the best choice for merchants who want to focus on selling rather than configuring features. At $29 per month, the Basic plan is competitive and the time saved on setup justifies the cost for most small to mid-size stores. The lack of sales thresholds also means pricing stays predictable as you scale.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for B2B, wholesale, and feature-rich stores: BigCommerce.</strong> BigCommerce's built-in features, native B2B capabilities, zero transaction fees, and stronger SEO control make it the best choice for merchants who need more functionality without paying for apps. The Plus plan at $79.95 per month offers customer groups, stored cards, and quote management that would require Shopify Plus at $2,300 per month.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for stores processing high volume on third-party gateways: BigCommerce.</strong> A store doing $100,000 per month on PayPal saves $2,000 per month in Shopify transaction fees by using BigCommerce. The tradeoff is the annual sales threshold, but for merchants who want predictable per-transaction economics, BigCommerce is the better choice.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended approach:</strong> Start on Shopify if you are a first-time merchant, want to launch quickly, or prioritize a polished checkout experience. Start on BigCommerce if you sell B2B, need built-in features without app fees, or want stronger SEO control. Most merchants pick the right platform within the first 30 days of a free trial, and both platforms handle growth well into eight figures.</p>
    </div>

