    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Shopify better than BigCommerce for beginners?</h3>
    <p>Yes, Shopify is better for most beginners because its setup flow is faster and its theme editor is more intuitive. You sign up, pick a theme, add products, and your store is live within an hour. BigCommerce has a steeper learning curve because it exposes more built-in features such as product variants, faceted search, and complex shipping rules in the default dashboard. For a first-time store owner who wants the simplest path to launch, Shopify wins. If you want more functionality out of the box without installing apps, BigCommerce is the better choice.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Shopify or BigCommerce?</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce is slightly cheaper at the entry level because its Standard plan at $29.95 per month includes features that Shopify only offers on higher tiers, such as professional reports, gift cards, and real-time carrier shipping. Shopify Basic at $29 per month is cheaper on paper, but you often need to add paid apps to match BigCommerce's built-in functionality. BigCommerce also charges no transaction fees on any gateway, while Shopify charges up to 2% if you do not use Shopify Payments. For stores processing high volume on third-party gateways, BigCommerce is the cheaper option overall.</p>

    <h3>Which platform has better SEO, Shopify or BigCommerce?</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce has a slight edge in SEO because it gives you more control over URL structure, automatic redirects, and structured data. BigCommerce also includes built-in features like microdata, custom URLs, and a robust blog engine on every plan. Shopify SEO is solid and handles the basics well, but it forces URL structures such as /products/ and /collections/ that you cannot remove. For most stores the SEO difference is small, and content quality matters more than platform choice. If SEO is your primary growth channel, BigCommerce offers more granular control.</p>

    <h3>Can I migrate from BigCommerce to Shopify?</h3>
    <p>Yes, you can migrate from BigCommerce to Shopify. Shopify offers a free Store Importer app that transfers products, customers, and order history directly from BigCommerce. The process typically takes a few hours for stores under 1,000 products. You will need to manually redirect old URLs to preserve SEO, and some custom BigCommerce fields or product options may not map cleanly to Shopify. Many merchants hire a migration specialist for stores with complex data, custom functionality, or large catalogs above 5,000 products.</p>

    <h3>Which platform handles high traffic better, Shopify or BigCommerce?</h3>
    <p>Both platforms handle high traffic well because they are fully hosted cloud platforms with unlimited bandwidth and automatic scaling. Shopify has a slight edge during extreme traffic spikes such as Black Friday because its infrastructure is more battle-tested at scale, powering over 4 million stores. BigCommerce also scales automatically and has handled major enterprise brands, but it enforces annual sales thresholds on each plan, which means a sudden traffic surge may push you into a higher pricing tier. For flash sales and viral campaigns, Shopify is the safer choice because there are no sales thresholds.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Shopify and BigCommerce together?</h3>
    <p>It is not practical to run both platforms for the same store, but some businesses use Shopify for one brand and BigCommerce for a separate brand or regional catalog. A more common approach is to pick one platform based on your business model and stick with it, because maintaining two ecommerce systems doubles your operational overhead. If you are evaluating both, start with a free trial of each, build a small test store, and migrate to the one that fits your product catalog, budget, and growth strategy. Most merchants choose one platform within the first month.</p>

